<TopicPage slug="subjects/linguistics/expert/computational-linguistics">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the expert tier of Linguistics, and three earlier lessons carry you here. From the
    intermediate tier, <em>Syntax: trees and rules</em> supplied the parser's grammar machinery
    that the rule-based pipeline uses, and <em>Semantics and pragmatics</em> defined the
    meaning representations the pipeline outputs. From the advanced tier,
    <em>Psycholinguistics and acquisition</em> prepared the surprisal connection — reading
    difficulty measured in eye-milliseconds — that turns language models into cognitive
    hypotheses. What is new here is the machine: how language became data, and how data became
    models.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>explain how corpora, Zipf's law and Shannon's entropy framework made language countable and predictable;</li>
    <li>trace the arc from rule-based NLP through statistical machine translation to embeddings and transformers;</li>
    <li>read perplexity and word error rates critically, including the equity problems benchmarks hide;</li>
    <li>state what corpora revealed about language itself — lexis behaving grammatically, surprisal linking models to psycholinguistics;</li>
    <li>assess the low-resource problem and the probing roles linguists occupy in the model era.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Computational linguistics (NLP) is <strong>language processing by machine</strong> — from
    the rule-based pioneers through the statistical revolution to today's transformer models.
    It is simultaneously applied linguistics and AI's hardest text problem, and it feeds
    discoveries back into linguistic theory: the field that made "corpus" a scientific term
    also made "language model" a household one. The unit's two topics are its two halves — the
    data (corpora, and what modelling language from data revealed about language itself) and
    the technology (the pipeline that turned machine translation into large language models).
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="rule-based, statistical, embeddings, transformers" widget="equationStep" config={compolingCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the four panels are the field's history compressed into one
    argument, and each step explains why the previous one stopped working. Panel 1 shows the
    rule era's brittleness; panel 2 answers it with probability learned from data — note the
    half-truth about firing linguists, because the linguists' treebanks were quietly
    load-bearing. Panel 3 is the pivotal change of representation: meaning as position in
    geometric space. Panel 4 shows one architecture swallowing the separate tasks, which sets
    up the lesson's closing question — what did statistical learning actually learn about
    grammar?
  </p>
  <p><strong>So far:</strong> computational linguistics runs on two halves — the data and the technology — and both trace back to one move: treating language as something that can be counted. The next section follows the data half, from the first million-word corpus to the equity problems of modern speech systems.</p>

  <h2>Corpora and the computational modelling of language</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The corpus as scientific instrument.</strong> The field began by making language
    countable: the Brown Corpus (1961, one million words of balanced American English) was the
    first machine-readable standard; the British National Corpus (1994, 100 million words)
    added scale and annotation; the web blew the ceiling off — today's training corpora run to
    trillions of tokens crawled from Common Crawl. Corpus linguistics' founding discovery is
    Zipf's law: word frequency falls roughly as 1/rank ("the" is about 7% of running English
    text; the top 100 words cover roughly half of it) — a scaling law so robust it shapes
    every model that follows. Annotation layers (part-of-speech tags, parses, the Penn
    Treebank) turned corpora into supervision for statistical learning.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Shannon's inheritance.</strong> Claude Shannon (1948) supplied the theoretical
    frame: language as a probabilistic process, its information content measurable in bits
    (his experiments estimated English at roughly one bit per character of true entropy), and
    the n-gram model — predict the next word from the previous n — as the baseline. Perplexity
    (how surprised the model is, on average, by real text) became the field's fundamental
    metric: every subsequent breakthrough is partly a perplexity story.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What corpora taught linguistics.</strong> The surprise was theoretical: lexis
    behaves grammatically. Sinclair's idiom principle — words co-occur in semi-fixed
    patterns far more than generative rules predicted — built the COBUILD dictionary (1987)
    straight from corpus evidence and pushed lexicography from introspection to data.
    Collocation statistics exposed meaning structure (which words gravitate to which), and
    <em>surprisal theory</em> (Hale 2001, Levy 2008) closed the loop with psycholinguistics:
    reading difficulty, measured eye-millisecond by eye-millisecond, tracks the model's
    surprise — the language model as a cognitive hypothesis. Distant reading (Moretti) is
    the same move for literature: corpora as the new archive.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The knowledge-resource layer.</strong> Between corpora and models sits a quieter
    infrastructure built largely by hand: WordNet (Princeton, from 1985 — a lexical database
    organising well over 100,000 English words into synonym sets linked by hyponymy, meronymy
    and antonymy), FrameNet's frame-semantic roles, and the treebanks and propbanks that label
    structure at scale. They powered the rule era's semantic processing, supervised the
    statistical era's word-sense disambiguation, and survive into the LLM era as evaluation
    gold standards — the rare artefacts every paradigm has found usable, which says something
    about what linguistic knowledge looks like independently of how it is acquired.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "corpora are neutral data — just what people say." They
    are selections with biases: written over spoken, formal over casual, English-dominant,
    web-scraped, and increasingly model-generated (synthetic text feeding back into training
    sets). Every corpus finding is a finding about that selection; the field's growing
    subfield of data documentation exists precisely because the neutrality assumption broke.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Speech: the oldest success story.</strong> Automatic speech recognition shows the
    whole arc in one task: Hidden Markov Models (1970s–80s) treated speech as a statistical
    sequence problem and reached usable dictation by the 1990s (word error rates around
    25–30% on conversational telephone speech); end-to-end neural models (2015 onward)
    crossed the roughly 5% error band that approximates human transcription on benchmarks.
    Synthesis travelled the same road (concatenated samples to neural voices indistinguishable
    in short clips). The linguistic residue matters: acoustic models still implicitly encode
    phonetics — phones, coarticulation, prosody — learned rather than hand-specified, which
    makes speech one of the cleanest cases where the machine rediscovers the linguist's
    categories from data.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "speech recognition is a solved problem — the benchmarks say
    so." Benchmarks measure their own conditions: Stanford's 2020 audit of five major
    commercial systems (Koenecke and colleagues, PNAS) found average word error rates of about
    35% for Black speakers against 19% for white speakers — nearly double — with African
    American English's underrepresentation in training data the likely driver. "Solved" is a
    claim about test conditions; deployed systems serve populations the benchmark never
    sampled, which is why error-rate equity rather than headline accuracy is becoming the
    field's honest metric.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The equity problem.</strong> Of the world's roughly 7,000 living languages, only
    a few dozen have the corpora, treebanks and translation pairs modern NLP runs on; most
    have less than a Wikipedia. The consequences compound — no data, no models; no models,
    no tools; no tools, less digital life in the language, so less future data. Community
    movements (Masakhane for African languages, indigenous-language institutes partnering
    with labs) and low-resource techniques (cross-lingual transfer from high-resource
    relatives, active community collection) are the field's response — and its clearest case
    where the engineering decision (which languages get models) is a cultural one.
    The mirror image is the high-resource languages' data-exhaustion problem: frontier-scale
    training has already scraped the web's text repeatedly, making data quality, licensing and
    synthetic data the active frontier of corpus work.
  </p>
  <p><strong>So far:</strong> the data half of the field made language countable — Zipf's law and Shannon's entropy gave the frame, corpora and treebanks gave the supervision — and exposed two honest limits: corpora are biased selections, and the resources are wildly unevenly distributed. Now the technology half: what happened when the hand-built rules gave way to learning.</p>

  <h2>From language technology to language models</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The rule-based era (1950s–1980s).</strong> Machine translation opened the field
    with the 1954 Georgetown experiment (60 Russian sentences translated automatically, and
    breathless press coverage) — followed by the sobering ALPAC report (1966), which found
    progress poor and funding died for two decades. Hand-built systems (parsers, semantic
    networks, ELIZA's 1966 pattern-matched therapy chatbot) proved the point that still
    defines the field: linguistic rules encode the exceptions their authors never anticipated,
    and coverage grows combinatorially worse, not better, with each new rule.
  </p>
  <p>
    ELIZA deserves its own sentence, because Weizenbaum's reaction became a field-level
    warning. His 1966 program matched patterns and reflected them back ("I feel sad" →
    "I'm sorry to hear you are sad") — and users confided in it, knowing exactly how it
    worked. Weizenbaum spent the rest of his career alarmed by what he had demonstrated:
    people will attribute understanding to almost anything that speaks. The "ELIZA effect"
    is now the name of that bias, and it frames every debate about chatbot companionship,
    customer-service "AI" and large language models — the effect preceded the technology
    by half a century, which is the point.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The philosophical frame the field grew inside.</strong> Turing's imitation game
    (1950) replaced "can machines think?" with an operational test — if a text exchange is
    indistinguishable from a human's, the question dissolves into measurement — and Searle's
    Chinese Room (1980) answered back: symbol manipulation alone yields no understanding, since
    a rule-follower inside the room passes the test while understanding nothing. NLP has lived
    between the two ever since: every evaluation is a proxy for understanding, and every proxy
    has known gaps — which is why the discipline's answer to the philosophy is methodological
    (probe, measure, report limits) rather than metaphysical. The 2020s debate about whether
    large language models "really" understand is the same exchange, with better opponents on
    both sides.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The statistical revolution (1990–2017).</strong> IBM's models (Brown and
    colleagues, 1990) learned translation probabilities from parallel corpora (the Canadian
    Parliament's bilingual Hansard as the canonical dataset); phrase-based systems and the
    BLEU metric (2002) industrialised evaluation; Google Translate (2006) shipped statistical
    MT at scale. Fred Jelinek's quip — "every time I fire a linguist, performance goes up" —
    is the era's half-truth: what actually worked was linguistics-free learning over
    linguistically structured data, with the linguists' resources (treebanks, lexicons)
    quietly load-bearing. Then embeddings changed the representation: word2vec (2013) placed
    words as vectors where geometry encoded meaning (king − man + woman ≈ queen), ELMo and
    BERT (2018) made the vectors contextual — one word, many senses, computed per sentence.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The transformer era (2017–now).</strong> "Attention Is All You Need" (Vaswani and
    colleagues, 2017) replaced recurrence with attention — every token attending to every
    other, computed in parallel — and scaling laws did the rest: GPT-3 (2020, 175 billion
    parameters) showed capability emerging with scale; the Chinchilla analysis (2022) found
    the compute-optimal ratio (train on roughly 20 tokens per parameter) and redirected the
    industry toward data quality; instruction tuning and RLHF aligned the models into usable
    assistants. Translation, summarisation, dialogue, coding — one architecture, task-switched
    by prompt. The 2020s' research question flipped: linguists now <em>probe</em> the models —
    what syntax, semantics and world knowledge did statistical learning actually acquire?
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What linguists do now.</strong> Three live roles. Probing and evaluation: designing
    minimal-pair tests (agreement across long dependencies, garden-path sentences,
    NPI licensing) that separate grammatical competence from memorisation, and building
    benchmarks whose answers the models cannot have scraped. Documentation and equity:
    the large majority of the world's ~7,000 languages have no usable corpus — low-resource
    NLP (massively multilingual models, community data collection) is where the field's
    social stakes are highest. And theory: surprisal, information-theoretic accounts of
    production, and the debate over whether next-word prediction is a theory of language or
    a telescope pointed at one.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "language models just predict the next word, so they can't
    really know grammar." Prediction under uncertainty is the hard part: to assign low
    surprise to "the keys to the cabinet are…", a model must track a plural subject across
    a prepositional phrase — exactly the hierarchical dependency generative grammar
    formalised. Whether the models do it the same way humans do is the real (open) question;
    "just next-word prediction" is a description of the training objective, not of what the
    objective forces the model to learn.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "it is all just statistics, so the models understand
    nothing" — and its mirror image, "they pass every test, so they understand like we do."
    Both overreach. "Just statistics" describes the training mathematics, not what the
    mathematics learns (evolution is "just" mutation and selection, and still builds eyes);
    benchmark performance is compatible with brittle pattern-matching, as adversarial and
    contaminated evaluations keep showing. The defensible position — and the field's working
    one — is instrument-based: probe specific capabilities, report specific failures, and
    resist the one-word verdict.
  </p>
  <p><strong>So far:</strong> the technology half traced one continuous story — rules that could not cover their own exceptions, probabilities learned from parallel text, meaning turned into geometry, then attention over tokens — with the research question flipping at the end from building models to probing them. Before the deeper arguments, check that the eras and their metrics are straight.</p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Order the three eras — rule-based, statistical, transformer — and name the change of representation that separates the statistical era's vectors from the transformer era's.</li>
    <li>A deployed speech system reports 12% word error rate on its benchmark average, yet one community's rate is 35%. What does the lesson say this does to a "solved problem" claim?</li>
    <li>Why does the lesson call perplexity the field's fundamental metric, with every breakthrough "partly a perplexity story"? Connect Shannon's baseline to the transformer era.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Rules (1950s–1980s, hand-built and combinatorially brittle),
    then statistics (1990–2017, Brown et al. 1990 learned translation probabilities from parallel
    corpora), then transformers (2017–now). The representation change: word2vec's static vectors
    became contextual — ELMo and BERT compute one word, many senses, per sentence — and attention
    lets every token consult every other. (2) Benchmarks measure their own conditions: the
    Koenecke audit found roughly 35% against 19% for white speakers, with underrepresentation in
    training data the likely driver. "Solved" is a claim about test conditions — error-rate
    equity, not headline accuracy, is the honest metric. (3) Shannon framed language as a
    probabilistic process and gave the n-gram baseline; perplexity measures a model's average
    surprise at real text, and statistical MT, embeddings and attention can all be read as
    lowering that surprise — which is what makes progress comparable across architectures that
    share no machinery.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Corpora made language countable; Zipf's law and Shannon's entropy frame still shape every model that follows.</li>
    <li>Perplexity is the field's fundamental metric — each era's breakthrough is partly a story of lowered surprise.</li>
    <li>Corpora are selections with biases; data documentation became a subfield because the neutrality assumption broke.</li>
    <li>The rule era's lesson: linguistic rules encode the exceptions their authors never anticipated.</li>
    <li>Statistical learning worked over linguistically structured data — treebanks and lexicons stayed load-bearing.</li>
    <li>Embeddings turned meaning into geometry, and attention produced one architecture for all tasks.</li>
    <li>Most of the world's roughly 7,000 languages lack usable corpora; low-resource NLP is where engineering meets cultural stakes.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The subject's other expert lesson, <strong>Generative grammar</strong>, holds the theory
    half of the bargain: the hierarchical dependencies that this lesson's probing experiments
    test models against. Read the two as one dialogue — the formal grammar states what
    competence should look like, and computational linguistics measures how far learning from
    data alone reaches it.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The evaluation problem.</strong> Perplexity correlates with quality but is
    gameable; benchmarks (GLUE, SuperGLUE, the WMT translation shared tasks) drove a decade
    of progress until contamination — test data leaking into training scrapes — made "state
    of the art" ambiguous. The current answers: held-out private test sets, human preference
    panels, and adversarial or dynamic benchmarks. The deep issue is unsolved: we lack a
    theory of what task performance means about linguistic knowledge — evaluation is where
    NLP's science is weakest exactly as its deployment is strongest.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The linguistic theory stakes.</strong> Two programs now compete on the same
    terrain. If usage-based learning from raw text suffices, the poverty-of-stimulus argument
    weakens and construction grammar's fortunes rise; if models systematically fail where
    children succeed (novel compositional generalisation, low-data acquisition), the
    built-in-structure hypothesis gains ground. The empirical tests are running in both
    directions — models trained on child-sized data slices (BabyLM-style challenges) being
    the sharpest instrument — and either outcome rewrites a 60-year debate.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> Will synthetic data (models training on models' output)
    cause drift or collapse — the model-collapse literature is young? Can multimodal models
    ground meaning in perception enough for semantics to escape text-only circularity? And
    who owns language data — the copyright cases moving through courts will decide what
    corpora the next decade trains on. The subfield that began by counting one million words
    is now negotiating the terms of its own fuel supply.
  </p>

